                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_coo:           `<strong>You anchored on the buyer's outcome.</strong> The COO signs the €480,000 and wants measurable operational impact in six months — building the COO's outcome into the spine, with HR's culture and the managers' practicality in the how, is exactly how you reconcile three “clients” into one fundable objective.`,
                    coach_r1_hr:            `<strong>Beautiful transformational-leadership journey.</strong> The COO reads it and says: “I asked for measurable operational impact in six months. This is a culture programme. What does my factory floor look like differently in Q1?” You designed for the sponsor and lost the buyer. Reconcile the two without doubling the budget.`,
                    coach_r1_managerlight:  `<strong>A practical toolkit will please the managers</strong> — but a light toolkit alone won't read as the leadership programme the COO is buying, and it leaves HR's culture ambition unaddressed. Relevance is necessary, not sufficient; anchor it to the buyer's outcome.`,
                    coach_r1_allthree:      `<strong>You can't maximise all three inside €480,000.</strong> Promising transformation AND six-month impact AND a full practical journey at this budget over-scopes the programme and breaks the margin before you start. Scoping is choosing the primary objective, not refusing to choose.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp decode.</strong> You anchored on the COO's measurable outcome AND treated the manager survey as a design input. That is client-centric scoping — the journey is built backwards from the business need and from what these time-poor managers will actually use.`,
                    coach_r1_slide:         `<strong>The survey was your relevance data, not a slide.</strong> Quote it then design three days of frameworks and a strategy lecture, and the managers rate it 5/10, the COO sees the scores, and there's no Cohort 2. Let the survey shape the design, not decorate the proposal.`,
                    coach_r1_lowgap:        `<strong>You glossed over the €46k gap.</strong> The first costing is €430k against a €384k ceiling. Pretending the gap is small now means an over-margin proposal later. Name the binding constraint honestly in the decode.`,
                    coach_r2_targeted:      `<strong>That is the portfolio done right.</strong> Bespoke where client-fit pays — execution and the capstone — and proven open modules elsewhere, with real coaching budget. You bought fit where it matters without blowing the €90k envelope.`,
                    coach_r2_everything:    `<strong>You custom-built all five modules — and spent €132k designing a €90k envelope.</strong> There's now €42k bleeding into the delivery ceiling and nothing left for faculty. Bespoke everywhere isn't premium, it's bankruptcy. Where does custom content actually earn its cost?`,
                    coach_r2_catalogue:     `<strong>Open everywhere protects the budget but not the client.</strong> A programme stitched entirely from catalogue modules reads as generic — the buyer paying a custom price for an open product walks to the boutique competitor. Build bespoke where fit pays.`,
                    coach_r2_spread:        `<strong>You spread the €90k thinly across low-leverage modules.</strong> Strategy and leading-change framing matter less to this COO than execution, coaching and a real capstone. Concentrate the design euros where client-fit and relevance are actually created.`,
                    coach_r2_nocoaching:    `<strong>You starved the coaching/360 module.</strong> Coaching is where time-poor managers convert frameworks into changed behaviour — it's the part the COO's six-month impact rests on. Underfunding it now quietly caps both fit and relevance.`,
                    coach_r3_balancedmix:   `<strong>A disciplined open share inside the cap.</strong> Using proven open modules for 25–40% of contact hours pulls real cost out while keeping the programme genuinely custom. That's the open/custom trade made deliberately, not by accident.`,
                    coach_r3_allcustom:     `<strong>Almost no open substitution keeps fit high but gives you no cost relief.</strong> With nothing proven in the mix you're carrying the full bespoke cost into a €46k gap — fine for fit, but you'll have to close the whole gap on the faculty and coaching side.`,
                    coach_r3_overcap:       `<strong>Above 40% open it stops being a custom programme.</strong> The school won't let you bill custom pricing for a mostly-open product, and the buyer feels the generic content. You saved cost and lost the very thing they're paying you for.`,
                    coach_r3_cutcoaching:   `<strong>You closed the gap by halving coaching.</strong> You made margin — and built a programme that underwhelms 120 managers and exhausts the rest. The framework agreement evaporates after Cohort 1. Which cut protects margin <em>without</em> gutting what the client is buying?`,
                    coach_r3_holdtrim:      `<strong>Holding price and trimming scope closes the gap on paper.</strong> But a thinner programme with fewer contact days reads as less for the same price — the buyer notices, and learner-relevance drops. A cleaner solve mixes a bespoke→open swap with core-faculty self-delivery.`,
                    coach_r3_safesolve:     `<strong>That's the margin bridge that protects the deliverable.</strong> One bespoke→open swap plus core-faculty self-delivery clears the €46k gap without touching coaching or thinning the journey. You hit the 20% floor and kept the thing the client is buying intact.`,
                    coach_r4_ninety:        `<strong>You priced the guarantee, not the hope.</strong> Costing the per-learner economics on 90 seats means the margin holds even if internal uptake lands at 94 — and the extra seats become upside, not the difference between making and missing the floor.`,
                    coach_r4_hundredfive:   `<strong>Pricing 105 splits the difference — and the risk.</strong> If uptake lands at 94 your fixed design spreads over fewer learners than you priced, and margin drifts toward the floor. Defensible, but you've priced some of the hope.`,
                    coach_r4_onetwenty:     `<strong>You priced full capacity.</strong> The client guaranteed 90; internal uptake lands at 94. Your fixed design cost now spreads over 94, not 120, and your margin drops below the 20% floor on the day you sign. Did you price the guarantee or the hope?`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That's a panel-ready defence.</strong> A survey-led design and an honest margin bridge — bespoke where fit pays, proven modules where it doesn't — answers “too generic / too expensive” with substance instead of a discount. You held the value and the floor.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>You caved on price to match the boutique.</strong> A reflex discount to win the bid pushes you straight through the 20% floor and sets a precedent that erodes the P&L on every future deal. Defend the value, don't discount the margin.`,
                    coach_r4_morecustom:    `<strong>Promising more bespoke build on the spot looks premium</strong> — and quietly pushes cost back over the ceiling you just worked to clear. Adding scope under panel pressure is how a won bid becomes an unprofitable contract.`,
                    coach_r4_rhetoric:      `<strong>“Impact is cultural and hard to quantify” is not what the COO asked for.</strong> The buyer wants measurable operational change in six months. A transformation narrative with no metrics reads as evasion — name the operational measures and the baseline, or lose the buyer.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
